Good. Both sprite files have the same structure:
```xml
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<svg xmlns="..." width="0" height="0">
  <defs>
    <symbol ...>...</symbol>
    ...
  </defs>
</svg>
```

For the extraction, I need the content inside `<defs>...</defs>`. But there might be a `<defs>` tag in the SVG root too (with attributes), and I only want the inner content.

Let me check: the `id` check in the Vite `load` hook. When Vite processes `import sprite from "./provider-icons/sprite.svg"`, the resolved `id` passed to `load` would be the absolute file path. So I should use `id.endsWith("provider-icons/sprite.svg")` and `id.endsWith("file-icons/sprite.svg")`.

Now let me also think about whether I should handle the XML declaration. The `<defs>...</defs>` content should not include the XML declaration or the outer SVG tags. My regex `/<defs[^>]*>([\s\S]*)<\/defs>/` should capture only the inner content.

Wait, I should be careful with the regex. The `?>` at the end matches a literal `>` after `defs`. Let me use a slightly better approach: `/<defs[^>]*>([\s\S]*)<\/defs>/` - this matches `<defs` followed by any non-`>` chars, then `>`, then captures everything (including newlines with `[\s\S]`) until `</defs>`.

But what if there are nested `<defs>` elements? E.g., a `<symbol>` might contain a `<defs>`. In SVG sprites, symbols typically don't contain `<defs>`, but they could. Let me check... Looking at the file content, the symbols only have `<path>` elements. So no nested `<defs>`.

Actually, looking at `file-icon.tsx`, the monochrome variant creates `<defs><mask>` at runtime - but the sprite SVG itself doesn't have them. So the sprites are safe.

Now, let me implement the plugin. I need to also handle a potential case: what if the regex doesn't match? That would happen if the SVG has no `<defs>` tags. In that case, I'd fallback to using the entire inner SVG content minus the XML declaration and outer `<svg>` tags.

Let me write the code:

```ts
function spriteInlinePlugin(): Plugin {
  return {
    name: "sprite-inline",
    async load(id) {
      if (!id.endsWith("provider-icons/sprite.svg") && !id.endsWith("file-icons/sprite.svg")) return

      const raw = await readFile(id, "utf-8")
      const defsMatch = raw.match(/<defs[^>]*>([\s\S]*)<\/defs>/)
      const symbols = defsMatch?.[1]?.trim() ?? raw
      const spriteId = id.endsWith("provider-icons/sprite.svg")
        ? "opencode-provider-icon-sprite"
        : "opencode-file-icon-sprite"

      return `
if (typeof document !== "undefined") {
  (function () {
    if (document.getElementById("${spriteId}")) return;
    var el = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "svg");
    el.id = "${spriteId}";
    el.setAttribute("aria-hidden", "true");
    el.setAttribute("width", "0");
    el.setAttribute("height", "0");
    el.style.position = "absolute";
    el.style.overflow = "hidden";
    el.innerHTML = ${JSON.stringify(symbols)};
    if (document.body) {
      document.body.appendChild(el);
    } else {
      addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", function () {
        document.body.appendChild(el);
      });
    }
  })();
}
export default "";
`
    },
  }
}
```

And add it to the plugins array in the Vite config.

Now let me verify: will this actually work? Let me trace through what happens:

1. `provider-icon.tsx` has `import sprite from "./provider-icons/sprite.svg"`
2. Vite resolves this to the file path
3. Our `load` hook intercepts and returns the JS module above
4. Vite treats this as the module's content
5. The module exports `""` as default
6. When the IIFE bundle executes, the module-level code runs, injecting the sprite into `document.body`
7. `ProviderIcon` renders `<use href="" + "#openai">` = `<use href="#openai">`
8. The browser resolves `#openai` against the injected SVG element with `<symbol id="openai">`

This should work! Let me implement it.

One thing I want to consider: the IIFE bundle might be quite large with the inline sprite. The provider icons sprite is ~282 KB / 1135 lines. With JSON.stringify, the string will be even larger (escaping). But this is necessary anyway - the symbols have to be somewhere.

Actually wait - can I use the raw string instead of JSON.stringify? No, because the SVG content contains characters that need escaping in JS strings (like quotes, backslashes, etc.). JSON.stringify handles this correctly.

Let me also consider: will the large string cause any issues with esbuild minification? It should be fine - esbuild handles large string literals.

One more thing: the provider-icons sprite has an XML declaration on line 1: `<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>`. My regex extracts from inside `<defs>`, which is AFTER the XML declaration and outer `<svg>` tag. So the extracted content won't include the XML declaration, which is correct.

OK, let me implement it now.

